Start and exit without the heap, apart from the dynamic linker's own.
A static program that does nothing began with 47 allocations in nine
size classes, and each class opens a slab of its own: nine mmaps of
512 KiB, each a system call, a region in the kernel and a page fault,
and as many unmapped when the program ends, in a program that is run
and waited for (fork, exec, wait) every time. What the C library
itself allocated before main is gone: 16 allocations in five classes are
left, the dynamic linker's (the main object, its TLS block, its
lists). Now: __cxa_atexit keeps its handlers in blocks that never
move, the first in the library's data with room for 32, as glibc's
`initial` block does, and takes from the heap only past it (it grew a
vector through three size classes, and a handler that registered
another while it ran wrote through a reference into the old, freed
vector); the locale's globals (the C locale and the starting locale,
whose tables of every category constructors built twice, with a string
for each, and the archive and the paths) are built the first time
something asks for them, and never destroyed, so no constructor runs
before main and no destructor is registered; a thread's table of key
values (16 KiB, cleared) is allocated by its first pthread_setspecific,
so that a thread that sets none takes none, and its exit does not look
at the 1024 keys four times; and environ points to the environment the
program started with, as in glibc, where each string was putenv()ed in
turn, into a growing array through as many size classes, comparing every
name with those before it (the first setenv() or putenv() copies it).
A name that is there twice is the first one's now, as in glibc, and
not the last one's. Handlers that atexit() functions register while
the exit runs are still not called, as before.

